1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,272
Picture a pair of shoppers: the first fills the cart
with organic food, the other buys whatever is cheapest.

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,320
Years later, the organic
shopper is in better health.

3
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:14,525
Was it the food, or the
kind of person who buys it?

4
00:00:15,533 --> 00:00:21,077
And behind that sits a second question: does
organic food lower your risk of cancer?

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:27,288
The channel Seeker makes one case: "it seems to
me that organic has lots of health benefits".

6
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:34,212
Dr. Jason West goes further, saying eating organic
"helps to prevent autoimmune and chronic diseases".

7
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:41,601
Harvard Health disagrees: "there isn’t clear evidence
that organic foods are also healthier for people".

8
00:00:42,133 --> 00:00:55,957
A systematic review found that, in observational studies, eating organic went along with lower rates of
metabolic syndrome, high body weight, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, infertility and birth defects, among other outcomes.

9
00:00:55,967 --> 00:01:04,535
The same review warns that organic eating often comes with a healthier diet
overall and less overweight, and that this is likely to sway those results.

10
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:16,423
A second review saw similar signals, for childhood allergies, adult overweight and non-Hodgkin
lymphoma, and concluded that it is not yet possible to say whether organic food causes any of them.

11
00:01:16,733 --> 00:01:30,605
Its authors point out that people who prefer organic also eat more fruit, vegetables, whole grains and legumes and less
meat, and they expect those people to have less diabetes and cardiovascular disease as a consequence of that diet.

12
00:01:30,633 --> 00:01:36,033
What about trials, where researchers change
what people eat and watch what happens?

13
00:01:36,533 --> 00:01:44,501
Those trials have been short, and have mostly measured stand-in
markers rather than disease itself, with limited positive results.

14
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:57,829
Another review counted 17 studies in people, and only 3 of them looked at actual illness: they
found no significant differences in allergic conditions or in symptomatic Campylobacter infection.

15
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:07,049
Studies in adults that measured nutrients and other markers in blood,
urine, breast milk and semen found no clinically meaningful differences.

16
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:17,027
And a report on organic foods found no well-powered human study that directly
shows a health benefit, or protection from disease, from eating an organic diet.

17
00:02:17,033 --> 00:02:22,073
So no health benefit of eating organic
has been proven in the people who eat it.

18
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:38,556
Not proven is not the same as ruled out: studies consistently find fewer pesticide breakdown products in the urine of people on an
organic diet, and the first review's authors think it possible that lower exposure, kept up over time, could turn into health benefits.

19
00:02:38,567 --> 00:02:44,111
If a benefit is there, could it show up in the
outcome people fear most: who gets cancer?

20
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:52,741
Tim Spector, on the ZOE channel, says one study found "the
organic food group were protected by about 25 percent".

21
00:02:52,767 --> 00:03:04,047
A Dana-Farber Cancer Institute video describes a large study of British women that found "organic
food intake was not associated with a decreased cancer risk for the cancers that they evaluated".

22
00:03:04,567 --> 00:03:21,655
The NutriNet-Santé cohort followed 68,946 adults for an average of 4.6 years, and after adjusting for other factors, the
quarter who scored highest for eating organic had a 25 percent lower rate of cancer than the quarter who scored lowest.

23
00:03:21,967 --> 00:03:27,919
In absolute terms, though, the difference in the
share of people who got cancer was 0.6 percent.

24
00:03:28,433 --> 00:03:35,897
The lower risk showed up for non-Hodgkin lymphoma and
postmenopausal breast cancer, and not for other types of cancer.

25
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:41,372
In the United Kingdom, the Million Women Study
followed its participants for about 9 years.

26
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:51,396
Women who said they usually or always ate organic had no lower rate of cancer overall
than women who said they never did, and a slightly higher rate of breast cancer.

27
00:03:51,400 --> 00:04:00,448
The one cancer that was lower was non-Hodgkin lymphoma, and the authors'
conclusion hedges even that: "except possibly for non-Hodgkin lymphoma".

28
00:04:00,467 --> 00:04:05,747
So these cohorts agree on lymphoma and point
in opposite directions on breast cancer.

29
00:04:05,767 --> 00:04:07,639
Why would the cohorts disagree?

30
00:04:08,167 --> 00:04:22,447
In NutriNet-Santé, the higher people scored for eating organic, the more likely they were to be women, better paid,
better educated and physically active, and the more fruit, vegetables, nuts and legumes and the less meat they ate.

31
00:04:22,467 --> 00:04:35,931
In the British study, women who usually or always ate organic were more likely to report strenuous exercise and
less likely to smoke or eat red and processed meat, though differences in socioeconomic status were smaller.

32
00:04:35,933 --> 00:04:51,557
The French team found that adding more diet measures did not change its result, but the systematic review from earlier notes that studies adjust
for diet in different ways, so a benefit may still be partly down to whole-food diets and healthier lives rather than the organic food itself.

33
00:04:51,567 --> 00:05:04,047
So the evidence does not show that organic food lowers cancer risk: one cohort found no overall drop,
and the other found a small absolute difference among people who also ate and lived differently.

34
00:05:05,067 --> 00:05:15,723
So the next time a label or a video promises that organic food will keep you well, ask one
question: was the benefit measured in people who were given the food, or in people who chose it?

35
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:21,369
Then ask what was counted, an illness or a
number on a blood test, and for how long.
